---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Blk from "../components/Blk.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import families from "../data/families.json";
import { byId } from "../lib/people.js";
const f = families.find((x) => x.surname === "Murdoch");
const shown = f.members.filter((m) => !m.living);
---
<Base
  title="The Murdochs"
  eyebrow={`${f.published} published people`}
  dek="From St Quivox in Ayrshire to Maloney Street, Toowoomba — a labourer, an engineer, and a man who died of a heart attack in the middle of a tennis game.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Audrey <strong>"Dell" Murdoch</strong> (1929–2022) was working in the Toowoomba
    branch of Union Trustees in 1948 when a young man was transferred up from Brisbane. She married
    him in 1952 and stayed married for just under fifty-eight years.
    <a href={u("/documents/")}>Her children tell the story →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Three generations, three hearts</h2>
    <div class="spine" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <div class="gen">
        <div><span class="yr">1824</span><span class="who">Francis Murdoch</span>
        <span class="whr"> · 1824–1909</span></div>
        <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem;max-width:72ch">Labourer, of St Quivox, Ayr. He
        married <strong>Marion Watt at Avondale, Lanarkshire, on 6 May 1854</strong> — the parish of
        which Strathaven is the town, and <em>1854, not the 1853 the family tree carries</em>. Their
        children were Marion Fleming, George, Janet, Jeanie, James and William. He died at Ayr in
        1909; he never left Scotland.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gen">
        <div><span class="yr">1856</span><span class="who">William Murdoch</span>
        <span class="whr"> · 1856–1918</span></div>
        <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem;max-width:72ch">Born <strong>St Quivox and
        Newton, Ayrshire, 7 October 1856</strong> — and the Scottish statutory register names his
        parents as <strong>Francis Murdoch and Marion Watt</strong>, which is how this line is
        documented rather than asserted. <strong>Engineer.</strong> The generation that emigrated.
        Married Catherine Jane Matson of Cornacreeve, County Monaghan. Died of a
        <strong>heart attack</strong> at Maloney Street, Toowoomba, 22 April 1918; buried Toowoomba
        Cemetery.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gen">
        <div><span class="yr">1894</span><span class="who">William James Fraser "Bill" Murdoch</span>
        <span class="whr"> · 1894–1946</span></div>
        <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem;max-width:72ch">Born Gympie, Queensland. Married
        Barbara "Goldie" Atwell at Brisbane, 11 April 1922. Died 26 May 1946 of a
        <strong>heart attack during a tennis game</strong>, aged fifty-one. His daughter Gayle
        recorded that he had been <em>a member of the Communist Party</em> — kept here as
        <span class="chip lore">family lore</span>, because it is remembered rather than
        documented, and it would be a straightforward thing to check.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gen">
        <div><span class="yr">1929</span><span class="who">Audrey "Dell" Murdoch</span>
        <span class="whr"> · 1929–2022</span></div>
        <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem;max-width:72ch">Born Toowoomba, 1 November 1929.
        Sixteen when her father died on the tennis court. Married Kenneth Lindsay D'Arcy at Toowoomba,
        5 April 1952. Died at Brisbane, 1 August 2022, aged ninety-two; interred at Centenary
        Memorial Gardens with her husband.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Blk label="1948, the Toowoomba office" title="&quot;He was a big disappointment&quot;">
    <blockquote>
      According to Mum, all the girls in the Toowoomba office were quite excited about the imminent
      arrival of the new young man from Brisbane. Likewise, Mum was full of anticipation and hope that
      a handsome heartthrob would walk through the office door that Monday morning. When she finally
      laid eyes on Dad, she clearly remembers thinking to herself — <em>'Ohhh'</em>.
      <strong>"He was a big disappointment,"</strong> she confided.
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— Gayle D'Arcy, eulogy for her
      father, March 2010</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <p>They were married four years later and stayed so for fifty-eight. They got to know each other
    at table tennis fixtures; she had the habit of getting the giggles every time she lost a point.
    Their daughter went on to be one of the best table tennis players in Queensland for years.</p>
  </Blk>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>The people</h2>
    <div class="grid three" style="margin-top:1rem">
      {shown.map((m) => {
        const o = byId.get(m.id);
        return (
          <a class="cardlink" href={u(`/people/${o ? o.slug : ""}`)}>
            <div class="nm">{m.name}</div>
            <p class="small" style="margin-top:.3rem">{m.life || "dates unknown"}
            {m.bornPlace ? ` · ${m.bornPlace.split(",")[0]}` : ""}</p>
          </a>
        );
      })}
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
